In this lesson, we'll look at function notation, a really handy bit of shorthand you'll use again and again. So instead of writing out a whole sentence like "the value of y when x equals two," mathematicians just write f of two. That's it. You're basically asking the function called f to spit out whatever it gives when you plug in two.

Here's what I mean. Say your function is f of x equals three x plus one. If you want to know what happens when x is two, you write f of two, and you read it as "f of two." You substitute the two in place of x, so three times two plus one, and you get seven. So f of two equals seven. Try x equals negative one, and you'd say f of negative one, which gives you three times negative one plus one, that's negative two. And if x is zero, you get f of zero equals one. That one is special, it's the y-intercept.

Now, why does this matter for modelling? A model names the relationship, and then instead of solving for y every single time, you just ask, what's f at this input? A couple of friendly conventions. The letter f is the most common function name, but you'll also see g, h, V, A, P, often chosen to hint at the quantity, like V for volume or P for price. And the input doesn't have to be x either. It could be t for time, r for radius, whatever keeps your model tidy. Now let's see it in action.